        <body>
            <div xml:id="d452">

                <pb facs="0543" n="445" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg445"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002 Manchuria/287: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Japan (Grew)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-12-20T13:00:00-05:00">December 20, 1932—1
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>189. Your 283, December 19, 4 p.m. In case of need, the Department perceives no
                    objection to the Consul using the address mentioned in the last paragraph of
                    your telegram under reference. The Department, however, sees no reason for
                    frequent formal communication and suggests that the Consul endeavor to transact
                    business on a personal and/or informal basis.</p>
                <p>In response to an inquiry some months ago from the Consul General at Mukden in
                    regard to the appropriateness of the Consulate General requesting from the local
                    customs authorities statistical information on behalf of American firms, the
                    Department replied that it considered it inadvisable for American consular
                    officers to attempt to obtain such information, even informally, from the
                    authorities of the new regime unless in a particular case the information
                    appeared to be essential to a legitimate American interest.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
